    "articles": [
      {
        "title": "Discovery and Naming",
        "body": "Agilisaurus louderbacki was described by Peng in 1990 from material found in the Lower Shaximiao Formation of Zigong, Sichuan, China. The generic name means 'agile lizard', reflecting its inferred fleet-footed lifestyle. The species name honours geologist George Louderback. The specimen is housed at the Zigong Dinosaur Museum and remains one of the most complete early ornithischian skeletons known from Asia."
      },
      {
        "title": "Anatomy",
        "body": "Agilisaurus was a slender, bipedal herbivore with long hind limbs suited to rapid locomotion. Its skeleton shares features with both basal ornithopods and more derived neornithischians, making its precise phylogenetic placement a subject of ongoing study. The well-preserved skull shows simple leaf-shaped teeth appropriate for processing plant material."
      },
      {
        "title": "Palaeoenvironment",
        "body": "The Shaximiao Formation represents a subtropical, seasonally wet environment of the Middle to Late Jurassic. Agilisaurus shared this environment with the sauropods Shunosaurus and Omeisaurus, the stegosaur Huayangosaurus, and the carnivore Gasosaurus. This diverse fauna reflects the high productivity of Jurassic East Asian ecosystems."
      },
      {
        "title": "Significance",
        "body": "As one of the most completely known basal neornithischians from the Jurassic of Asia, Agilisaurus provides an important reference point for understanding the early evolutionary radiation of ornithischian dinosaurs. Its relatively unspecialised anatomy makes it a useful outgroup for phylogenetic analyses of more derived ornithopods."
      }
    ],
