    "articles": [
      {
        "title": "Discovery and Naming",
        "body": "Dromaeosaurus albertensis was described by William Diller Matthew and Barnum Brown in 1922, based on material collected in the Red Deer River badlands of Alberta. It became the founding genus of Dromaeosauridae, a family name now applied to dozens of species worldwide. The holotype material includes a partial skull and lower jaws."
      },
      {
        "title": "Skull and Feeding Biology",
        "body": "Dromaeosaurus had a relatively deep, robust skull compared to many smaller dromaeosaurids, with strongly built teeth suggesting it could generate considerable bite force. Some researchers have proposed that its skull proportions are convergently similar to those of larger predators, allowing it to tackle prey disproportionate to its modest body size."
      },
      {
        "title": "The Sickle Claw",
        "body": "Like all dromaeosaurids, Dromaeosaurus bore a hyperextensible second toe armed with an enlarged, curved claw. Functional analyses of this structure in dromaeosaurids suggest it was used for gripping and restraining prey rather than primarily slashing, potentially allowing the animal to pin struggling victims while delivering bites."
      },
      {
        "title": "Wide Distribution",
        "body": "PBDB occurrence data documents Dromaeosaurus from multiple formations across North America as well as attributed teeth from Russia (Amur) and Uzbekistan (Navoi), though some referrals outside Canada and the USA should be treated cautiously given the fragmentary nature of disarticulated teeth. The North American record spans the Campanian to Maastrichtian."
      }
    ],
