    "articles": [
      {
        "title": "Discovery and Naming",
        "body": "Gobisaurus domoculus was described in 2001 by Vickaryous, Russell, Currie and Zhao from material collected in China. The species name 'domoculus' refers to the dome-shaped eyes of the skull. Its description added to the growing record of ankylosaurs from the Early Cretaceous of Asia."
      },
      {
        "title": "Anatomy and Armour",
        "body": "The skull of Gobisaurus is broad and low-slung, consistent with the ankylosaurid body plan. Although the full extent of body armour is not completely documented, Gobisaurus likely bore the characteristic fused osteoderms and a tail club seen in derived ankylosaurids. Skull proportions suggest a body length approaching 5 metres."
      },
      {
        "title": "Palaeoenvironment",
        "body": "The Early Cretaceous formations of Inner Mongolia and Henan record varied terrestrial environments ranging from floodplains to more arid settings. Gobisaurus would have been a low-browsing herbivore exploiting ground-level vegetation in these habitats, sharing its environment with theropods, sauropods, and other ornithischians."
      },
      {
        "title": "Scientific Significance",
        "body": "Gobisaurus helps fill a gap in the Asian ankylosaur record from the early part of the Cretaceous. Its phylogenetic placement within Ankylosauridae and its age inform hypotheses about when and where the ankylosaurid lineage diversified across Laurasia."
      }
    ],
