    "articles": [
      {
        "title": "Discovery and Naming",
        "body": "Neuquensaurus australis was first described by Richard Lydekker in 1893 from material collected in Patagonia, Argentina. It was subsequently reassigned among several genera before being recognised as a distinct saltasaurid. The genus name reflects the Neuquén region of Patagonia, and the species name australis means 'southern'."
      },
      {
        "title": "Osteoderm Armour",
        "body": "Neuquensaurus is one of the sauropods for which bony osteoderms — embedded scutes that form a mosaic of armour over the back — are directly documented. This feature, also seen in the closely related Saltasaurus, appears to be a derived characteristic of the saltasaurid lineage within titanosaurs and may have provided protection from large theropods."
      },
      {
        "title": "Ecology and Behaviour",
        "body": "PBDB ecospace data indicates that Neuquensaurus was gregarious, consistent with bone-bed and track-site evidence for titanosaur herding behaviour. Its herbivorous diet would have focused on the varied Late Cretaceous flora of Patagonia. The relatively modest body size compared to some titanosaurs may reflect ecological or island-effect constraints in its Patagonian range."
      },
      {
        "title": "Taxonomic History",
        "body": "Material referred to Neuquensaurus has had a complex history, having been placed in Titanosaurus, Laplatasaurus, and other genera at various times. PBDB credits Powell (1992) with the attribution currently in use. The species N. robustus was also named and its relationship to N. australis continues to be discussed in the context of individual variation and sexual dimorphism."
      }
    ],
