    "articles": [
      {
        "title": "Discovery and Naming",
        "body": "Nipponosaurus sachalinensis was described by Nagao in 1936 from a partial skeleton excavated on Sakhalin (then Japanese Karafuto). The genus name reflects Japan ('Nippon') and the species name refers to Sakhalin. The specimen represents a sub-adult individual, complicating direct size comparisons with adult members of related genera."
      },
      {
        "title": "Fossil Material and Limitations",
        "body": "The known material includes parts of the skull, vertebrae, limb elements and girdle bones, all from a single juvenile or sub-adult individual. The incompleteness and juvenile status of the holotype have made phylogenetic placement within Lambeosaurinae uncertain; some analyses support a close relationship with Asian lambeosaurines such as Olorotitan."
      },
      {
        "title": "Palaeogeography",
        "body": "The occurrence of a lambeosaurine hadrosaur on Late Cretaceous Sakhalin documents faunal connection between the Asian mainland and Sakhalin during the Santonian. This island-to-continent biogeographic link is relevant to understanding how hadrosaurs dispersed across the Late Cretaceous Pacific rim."
      },
      {
        "title": "Historical Significance",
        "body": "Nipponosaurus was one of only a handful of dinosaurs known from East Asian territory controlled by Japan in the prewar period and was important in establishing Japanese interest in palaeontology. It remains the only dinosaur species formally described from Sakhalin Island."
      }
    ],
