    "articles": [
      {
        "title": "Discovery and Naming",
        "body": "Parksosaurus warrenae was described by Charles Mortram Sternberg in 1937. The material was originally collected by William Parks, whose name the genus honours. The species name warrenae honours Mrs. H.D. Warren, a patron of the Royal Ontario Museum. The holotype was found in the Horseshoe Canyon Formation, an Early Maastrichtian deposit in central Alberta."
      },
      {
        "title": "Anatomy",
        "body": "The holotype preserves most of the skeleton in partial articulation, including skull elements, much of the vertebral column, ribs, limb bones and girdle elements. The skull is relatively small and triangular with a simple dental battery and horny beak. The hindlimbs are proportionally long, consistent with a cursorial (fast-running) lifestyle. The short forelimbs suggest habitual bipedality."
      },
      {
        "title": "Ecological Significance",
        "body": "Parksosaurus is one of very few non-hadrosaurid ornithopods documented from the latest Cretaceous of Laramidia (western North America), existing alongside hadrosaurs such as Edmontosaurus. The persistence of a small thescelosaurid alongside dominant hadrosaurs indicates ecological partitioning, likely involving differences in body size, foraging height or plant-type preferences."
      },
      {
        "title": "Taxonomic History",
        "body": "The genus has been variously placed in Hypsilophodontidae, Ornithopoda more broadly, and most recently within Thescelosauridae following the dissolution of the paraphyletic 'Hypsilophodontidae'. Its placement in Thescelosaurinae is based on shared derived characters of the skull and postcranium with Thescelosaurus. Wikipedia confirms Parksosaurus as one of the few non-hadrosaurid ornithopods from the end of the Cretaceous in North America."
      }
    ],
