    "articles": [
      {
        "title": "Discovery and Naming",
        "body": "Pinacosaurus was named by Charles W. Gilmore in 1933 based on material collected from Mongolia during the American Museum of Natural History expeditions of the 1920s. The type species is Pinacosaurus grangeri, honouring collector Walter Granger. Additional specimens have since been recovered by Mongolian-Soviet and later Mongolian-Japanese expeditions."
      },
      {
        "title": "Juvenile Aggregations",
        "body": "One of the most remarkable features of Pinacosaurus is the discovery of multiple juvenile individuals fossilised together in the same locality, interpreted by some researchers as evidence of herding or social grouping in young dinosaurs. This is relatively unusual in the ankylosaur fossil record and has informed debates about parental care and social behaviour in ornithischians."
      },
      {
        "title": "Anatomy and Armour",
        "body": "Pinacosaurus possessed the characteristic ankylosaur body armour of osteoderms embedded in the skin, along with a tail club used for defence. Its skull shows unusual multiple accessory nasal openings flanking the main naris, the function of which—whether related to thermoregulation, olfaction, or display—has not been conclusively determined."
      },
      {
        "title": "Ecological Setting",
        "body": "During the Late Cretaceous, the Gobi region was a semi-arid landscape of dunes and ephemeral water sources. Pinacosaurus shared its environment with small theropods, early mammals, and other dinosaurs of the Djadokhta fauna, browsing on low-growing vegetation with its herbivorous diet."
      }
    ],
