    "articles": [
      {
        "title": "Discovery and Naming",
        "body": "Saurornithoides mongoliensis was described by Henry Fairfield Osborn in 1924 from material collected by the American Museum of Natural History's Central Asiatic Expeditions led by Roy Chapman Andrews. It was one of the first small theropods described from Mongolia and contributed to early recognition of the diversity of Gobi Desert dinosaur faunas."
      },
      {
        "title": "Two Species",
        "body": "Two species of Saurornithoides are currently recognised. The type species S. mongoliensis, from the Djadokhta Formation (~75–71 Ma), is the smaller and older form. S. junior, from the Nemegt Formation (~72–66 Ma), is larger and is sometimes regarded as a separate genus. The relationship between these two species and other troodontids such as the North American Troodon continues to be studied."
      },
      {
        "title": "Large Brain and Senses",
        "body": "Troodontids including Saurornithoides are notable for having the highest known brain-to-body mass ratios among non-avian dinosaurs, comparable in some measures to modern birds. Large orbits suggest well-developed vision, and enlarged olfactory regions imply a keen sense of smell. These features have led to speculation about relatively complex behaviours, though direct evidence is limited."
      },
      {
        "title": "Ecology",
        "body": "Saurornithoides was likely an agile pursuit predator of small prey—lizards, early mammals, eggs, and insects—using its stereoscopic vision and sickle-shaped second toe claw. Its ecology in the Djadokhta Formation has been compared to that of modern hawks and mongooses, occupying a small-to-medium predator niche in the Gobi Desert ecosystem."
      }
    ],
