    "articles": [
      {
        "title": "Discovery and Naming",
        "body": "The first Beipiaosaurus remains were found in 1996 in the Yixian Formation of Liaoning, China, and formally described by Xu Xing, Tang Zhilu, and Wang Xiaolin in 1999 as Beipiaosaurus inexpectus, with the specific name meaning 'unexpected' to reflect its surprising mix of theropod and herbivorous features. Three specimens are currently reported from the region."
      },
      {
        "title": "Feathers and Integument",
        "body": "Beipiaosaurus preserves two types of filamentous integumentary structures: fine, hair-like protofeathers similar to those in other coelurosaurs, and unusually broad, elongated single-filament structures named EBFFs (elongated broad filamentous feathers). The EBFFs may have served a display function rather than insulation or flight. Feather colour has been inferred as brownish based on melanosome analysis."
      },
      {
        "title": "Diet and Anatomy",
        "body": "Unlike most theropods, Beipiaosaurus was herbivorous or omnivorous, as indicated by its leaf-shaped teeth, broad torso, and large claws suited to pulling vegetation. It belongs to the Therizinosauroidea, a clade of herbivorous coelurosaurs that convergently evolved a plant-eating lifestyle from carnivorous ancestors. Its forelimbs bore large, curved claws similar to those of its much larger relative Therizinosaurus."
      },
      {
        "title": "Scientific Importance",
        "body": "Before Yutyrannus was described in 2012, Beipiaosaurus was cited as one of the heaviest theropods known from direct evidence of feathering. Its combination of bird-like body plan, herbivorous adaptations, and diverse feather types made it a landmark specimen in the study of non-avian dinosaur integument and the evolution of feathers."
      }
    ],
