    "articles": [
      {
        "title": "Discovery and Naming",
        "body": "Buitreraptor gonzalezorum was described in 2005 by Peter Makovicky, Sebastián Apesteguía, and Federico Agnolin from the Candeleros Formation of Río Negro Province, Argentina. The species name honours the González family who facilitated access to the fossil site. Multiple specimens were recovered from the Buitrera locality, making it one of the best-known Gondwanan dromaeosaurids."
      },
      {
        "title": "Anatomy and Dentition",
        "body": "Buitreraptor had a notably elongated, flat snout with many small, recurved but unserrated teeth—unlike the serrated teeth typical of most dromaeosaurids. This dentition suggests dietary specialisation, possibly targeting fish, small vertebrates, or invertebrates rather than large prey. Its body was slender and lightweight, consistent with an agile pursuit predator."
      },
      {
        "title": "Gondwanan Biogeography",
        "body": "The placement of Buitreraptor within Unenlagiinae, a clade otherwise known from South America and Madagascar, is significant for understanding theropod dispersal. The presence of unenlagiines in Cenomanian Argentina, when the southern continents were still partially connected or recently separated, suggests either a Gondwanan origin for dromaeosaurids or an early dispersal across land bridges. This challenges the view that all dromaeosaurids radiated exclusively from Laurasia."
      },
      {
        "title": "Palaeoecology",
        "body": "The Candeleros Formation was a semi-arid floodplain environment during the Cenomanian, shared with large sauropods like Argentinosaurus and predators such as Giganotosaurus. Buitreraptor occupied a very different ecological niche as a small, agile predator, likely hunting alongside other small theropods and avoiding competition with the large carnivores."
      }
    ],
