    "articles": [
      {
        "title": "Discovery and Naming",
        "body": "Material now attributed to Cetiosaurus was first collected from Jurassic outcrops in England during the 1830s and described by Richard Owen in 1841, who initially considered it a giant crocodilian or marine reptile. The name Cetiosaurus ('whale lizard') reflects this misidentification. John Phillips formally described the best-known species, C. oxoniensis, in 1871 from more complete Oxfordshire material, establishing it as a land-dwelling sauropod."
      },
      {
        "title": "Historical Significance",
        "body": "Cetiosaurus was among the first sauropod dinosaurs to be described scientifically and played a key role in the gradual recognition of giant terrestrial dinosaurs. Early confusion over its nature—sea monster or land animal—reflects the early state of palaeontology in the 19th century. Its eventual recognition as a terrestrial herbivore contributed to the emerging concept of Dinosauria as a group including very large land animals."
      },
      {
        "title": "Anatomy and Classification",
        "body": "Cetiosaurus had a moderately long neck and tail, solid (not highly pneumatised) vertebrae compared to later sauropods, and robust limbs. Its solidly built vertebrae distinguish it from the hollow-boned titanosaurs and diplodocids, placing it among more basal eusauropods. Multiple species have been assigned to Cetiosaurus historically, but most are now considered nomina dubia or reassigned to other genera."
      },
      {
        "title": "Palaeoecology",
        "body": "During the Middle Jurassic, England lay at a subtropical latitude and was covered by shallow tropical seas interspersed with low islands. Cetiosaurus lived on these landmasses, browsing on vegetation. Its solid vertebrae and large body mass suggest it was less agile than the hollow-boned sauropods of the Late Jurassic, but it was one of the dominant large herbivores of its time and region."
      }
    ],
