    "articles": [
      {
        "title": "Discovery and Naming",
        "body": "Chirostenotes pergracilis was named by Charles Gilmore in 1924 from hand material found in the Dinosaur Park Formation of Alberta. Separately described as Macrophalangia (feet) and later linked to Caenagnathus (toothless jaws), the multiple names given to separate body parts of the same or similar animals led to decades of taxonomic confusion. The synonymy of these elements was progressively established through the second half of the 20th century."
      },
      {
        "title": "Anatomy and Distinctive Features",
        "body": "Chirostenotes had elongate, narrow fingers with large, curved claws—the feature that inspired its name. Like other caenagnathids and oviraptorids, it lacked teeth and had a beak-like mouth, which in caenagnathids was strengthened for crushing hard food items. Its body was lightly built and probably feathered. The skull, when eventually associated, showed a modest cranial crest."
      },
      {
        "title": "Diet and Ecology",
        "body": "As an omnivore, Chirostenotes likely consumed a varied diet including seeds, eggs, invertebrates, and small vertebrates. The toothless beak and powerful jaw muscles of caenagnathids suggest an ability to crack hard materials. Its ecological role was probably that of a generalist opportunist in the diverse Late Cretaceous ecosystems of North America."
      },
      {
        "title": "Taxonomic History and Referred Material",
        "body": "Chirostenotes has accumulated numerous referred specimens because isolated caenagnathid elements from across North America were broadly assigned to it. Some of this material likely belongs to distinct taxa, and ongoing revisionary work continues to refine the genus's definition. The 28 PBDB occurrences include material from multiple formations and time intervals, reinforcing the need for careful specimen-by-specimen reassessment."
      }
    ],
