    "articles": [
      {
        "title": "Discovery and Naming",
        "body": "Chungkingosaurus jiangbeiensis was described in 1983 by Dong Zhiming, Zhou Shiwu, and Zhang Yihong from material collected in the Shaximiao Formation near Chongqing (then Romanised as Chungking), Sichuan. The species name refers to the Jiangbei district of Chongqing. The material includes a partial skull and partial postcranial skeleton."
      },
      {
        "title": "Anatomy",
        "body": "Like other stegosaurines, Chungkingosaurus bore pairs of bony plates along its back and tail spikes (thagomizer). Its plates were relatively narrow and tall compared to the broader plates of Stegosaurus. The partial skull indicates a small head with leaf-shaped teeth suited for cropping vegetation. At 4 metres long, it was a smaller member of the stegosaur family."
      },
      {
        "title": "Sichuan's Remarkable Stegosaur Fauna",
        "body": "The Shaximiao Formation of Sichuan is globally significant for its stegosaur diversity. Alongside Chungkingosaurus, the formation has yielded Tuojiangosaurus and the more primitive Huayangosaurus, as well as diverse sauropods and theropods. This stegosaur assemblage suggests that the group diversified extensively in Asia during the Middle to Late Jurassic before spreading to other continents."
      },
      {
        "title": "Palaeoecology",
        "body": "The Shaximiao Formation ecosystem was a warm, well-watered floodplain environment during the Middle to Late Jurassic. Chungkingosaurus shared this landscape with large sauropods like Omeisaurus and Datousaurus, smaller ornithopods, and theropods such as Gasosaurus. As a small stegosaur, it likely fed on low-growing vegetation and used its tail spikes defensively against predators."
      }
    ],
