    "articles": [
      {
        "title": "Discovery and Naming",
        "body": "Citipati osmolskae was described in 2001 by James Clark, Mark Norell, and Rinchen Barsbold from specimens collected during American Museum of Natural History expeditions to the Gobi Desert in the 1990s. The genus name comes from the Citipati, skeletal figures in Tibetan Buddhist iconography, while the species honours Halszka Osmólska, a distinguished Polish palaeontologist. Earlier brooding specimens from the same region had been mistakenly identified as Oviraptor."
      },
      {
        "title": "Brooding Behaviour and Bird Connections",
        "body": "The most scientifically celebrated Citipati specimens are individuals found preserved in a brooding posture directly over clutches of eggs, with arms and body spread in a manner strikingly similar to brooding modern birds. These specimens provided some of the strongest direct evidence that non-avian theropods exhibited bird-like nesting and parental care. The posture indicates that the forelimbs were covered by feathers used to shelter the eggs, further reinforcing the close relationship between theropods and birds."
      },
      {
        "title": "Anatomy and Crest",
        "body": "Citipati had a prominent bony crest on its skull, a feature found in various oviraptorids. The crest may have been used in display behaviour or species recognition. Like all oviraptorids, it was toothless with a parrot-like beak, and its forelimbs were long with three-clawed hands. Its overall body plan was bird-like, with a short tail ending in a pygostyle-like structure, and it was almost certainly feathered."
      },
      {
        "title": "Djadochta Ecosystem",
        "body": "The Djadochta Formation represents a semi-arid dune environment in the Late Cretaceous Gobi Desert. Citipati shared this habitat with protoceratopsids, the small dromaeosaurid Velociraptor, the troodontid Saurornithoides, and the ankylosaur Pinacosaurus. The nesting behaviour of Citipati is interpreted as an adaptation to the variable, possibly unpredictable rainfall conditions of this desert environment."
      }
    ],
