    "articles": [
      {
        "title": "Discovery and Naming",
        "body": "Conchoraptor gracilis was described by Rinchen Barsbold in 1986 from multiple specimens collected from the Baruungoyot Formation of Mongolia. The genus name references the hypothesis that it used its strong beak to crack open mollusc shells, though this diet has not been confirmed. The species name gracilis means 'slender', referencing the lightly built skeleton. Multiple specimens of various sizes are known."
      },
      {
        "title": "Feathering and Sexual Dimorphism",
        "body": "Some specimens of Conchoraptor preserve evidence of feathers, confirming that it was feathered like other maniraptoran theropods. Notably, differences in plumage features between specimens have been interpreted as possible sexual dimorphism—one of the few cases where such a distinction has been proposed for a non-avian dinosaur. If confirmed, this would suggest that feather displays played a role in sexual selection in oviraptorids."
      },
      {
        "title": "Anatomy and Lack of Crest",
        "body": "Conchoraptor is distinguished from many oviraptorids by its lack of a cranial crest. This feature has been used to distinguish it from crested taxa like Oviraptor and Citipati. The beak was robust and the jaws were powered by large jaw muscles, suggesting an ability to process hard food items. Its forelimbs were long with three-fingered hands, and the overall build was lightly constructed."
      },
      {
        "title": "Baruungoyot Ecosystem",
        "body": "The Baruungoyot Formation was deposited in a desert environment during the Late Cretaceous Campanian. The fauna includes protoceratopsids, ankylosaurs, small theropods, and multiple oviraptorid genera. Conchoraptor coexisted with larger oviraptorids and likely occupied a slightly different ecological niche owing to its smaller size and distinct jaw morphology."
      }
    ],
